 ## Overview
-VaxNeRF is an acceleration strategy for Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) that reduces the number of point queries required during training and inference by restricting ray sampling to the **visual hull** $V$, defined as the maximum volume compatible with object silhouettes from multi-view images [S1]. Unlike the original NeRF, which samples points over the entire scene space using a two-stage coarse/fine MLP approach, VaxNeRF uses a single MLP with uniform sampling restricted to the visual hull, achieving 2-8x faster training without performance degradation [S1].
+VaxNeRF is an acceleration strategy for Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) that reduces the number of neural network queries during training and inference by restricting ray sampling to the **visual hull** $V$ [S1]. The visual hull is the maximum volume compatible with object silhouettes derived from multi-view foreground-background segmentation masks [S1]. Unlike the original NeRF, which samples points uniformly or adaptively across the entire scene bounding box using a coarse/fine two-stage MLP, VaxNeRF uses a single MLP with uniform sampling restricted to voxels inside $V$ [S1]. This approach achieves a training speedup of 2–8× without degrading rendering quality, provided coarse segmentation masks are available [S1].
 
