 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Speed:** VaxNeRF trains 2-8x faster than original NeRF [S1]. The speed gain depends on the visual hull size relative to the scene volume (e.g., factor of 4 or less for large objects like palace/ship, almost 8 for fine objects like steamtrain) [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Storing the visual hull in a $400 \times 400 \times 400$ voxel grid requires ~70 MB, while a basic NeRF MLP requires ~7 MB [S1].
-*   **Construction Time:** Visual hull construction takes around 10 seconds or less for tested input photo collections [S1].
-*   **Quality:** No loss of quality (PSNR) compared to original NeRF; in some cases, small improvements are observed [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple integration, minimal code changes, no quality degradation, significant training speedup [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires coarse foreground-background segmentation masks. Higher memory usage for the voxel grid compared to the MLP [S1].
+*   **Speed:** VaxNeRF trains 2–8× faster than the original NeRF [S1]. The speedup factor depends on the ratio of the visual hull volume to the total scene volume; larger objects (e.g., palace, ship) yield factors of 4 or less, while finer objects (e.g., steamtrain) yield factors up to 8 [S1].
+*   **Memory:** The voxel grid for $D=400$ requires approximately 70 MB of memory [S1]. This is significantly higher than the ~7 MB required for the original NeRF MLP [S1].
+*   **Quality:** No performance degradation in rendering quality (PSNR) compared to original NeRF [S1]. In some cases (e.g., steamtrain), slight PSNR improvements were observed, though convergence may require more steps [S1].
+*   **Construction Time:** Visual hull construction takes approximately 10 seconds or less for typical input photo collections [S1].
+*   **Requirements:** Requires coarse foreground-background segmentation masks for all input views [S1].
 
 ## Background
-VaxNeRF was proposed by Kondo et al. in 2021 [S1]. It addresses the time-consuming optimization process of original NeRF by restricting the search space to the visual hull, a classic concept in multi-view 3D reconstruction [S1]. The method is implemented in the open-source JAX implementation [S1] and evaluated on Synthetic-NeRF and Synthetic-NSVF datasets [S1].
+VaxNeRF was proposed by Kondo et al. in 2021 [S1]. It addresses the time-consuming optimization process of original NeRF by leveraging the classic multi-view 3D reconstruction concept of the visual hull [S1]. The method was implemented in JAX and evaluated on the Synthetic-NeRF and Synthetic-NSVF datasets [S1]. It is noted for its simplicity, requiring minimal code changes to existing NeRF implementations [S1].
 
